Z||000000||overlord||08-17-99||09:14 PM||||i was just hoping if someone knew if "blow-by" is anything serious?<p>i recently replaced my 2.5 with a newer one..and there is a small amount of engine oil coming up into the air cleaner..the guy at the shop said it was blow by.<p>is there something i can do to stop this oil coming up and wrecking my air filter?<p>p.s.  compression is fine in all cylinders and runs great..thnx guys
Z||000001||batboy||08-17-99||09:46 PM||||This blow-by issue has been discussed in a couple older discussions. There is a slim chance that your PVC valve is defective or bad, but usually blow-by is the result of a worn engine. Oil is being blown past the piston rings. This is common on high mileage engines. If this is the case, not much you can do except rebuild the motor. Give us the compression readings on all the cylinders and how often you have to add oil, this might help figure out the situation. Also, do a second compression check, but squirt some oil down the spark plug holes before this check. If the compression readings go up from the first set of numbers, then the rings have wear on them.
Z||000002||overlord||08-17-99||11:21 PM||||all 4 cyls. are 140.<br>and i havent had to add any oil yet since the engine was installed...oil doesnt always come up..and when it does not very much.<p>the guy at the shop..said it probably was engine wear cause i put new pcv stuff on when i bought the engine.<p>guess i wont buy a used engine again..o well
Z||000003||batboy||08-18-99||07:18 AM||||This has me perplexed. If the compression on all cylinders is 140 and you are not having to add oil, then the engine sounds like a good one (assuming you have good oil pressure). If indeed it is blow-by, I wouldn't worry about it too much, just change air filter more frequently than normal. How many miles have you put on it since the change over? If it's been less than a couple thousand miles, you probably haven't driven it enough to get a handle on the oil consumption situation. One last thing since you mentioned that the PCV valve was replaced recently. I have heard, especially on those 2.5 engines, that the incorrect PCV will sometimes "siphon" a little oil up into the air cleaner. It might be worth it to buy a real good one that you are 100% sure is the correct PCV valve. They're pretty cheap and would eliminate that possiblity.
Z||000004||DJRice||08-18-99||07:19 AM||||Back in the summer of 97 my dad found a deal on a cute little 84 2m4 with a bad engine.  (our first Fiero)  Later that year, we found a 84 2m4 that had a good engine and tranny, but the body was beat to hell.  So in December while on Christmas break from College, I swapped the motors and joila, I had a running Fiero.  I drove it for about 6 months before that engine gave up a rod.<p>That was my senior year in College, and I didnt have much Fiero time, but I graduated and got a real job, and on the weekends we work on the Fiero now. Just last week we finished replacing the engine in the 2m4 again.  Found a deal on an 85 that a dude thought needed a transmission.  Actually, It needed a shifter cable retaining ring.  I felt kinda bad, but not real bad, that it took only 10 minutes to diagnose and repair the car.  But it had no title, so we swapped the motors.
Z||000005||theogre||08-18-99||07:29 AM||||Blow by is caused by leaking rings letting conbustion gasses into the crankcase. This presurizes the crankcase and valve cover and since the only easy outlet is the breather tube, it blows oil out the tube.<p>Your new PCV valve could be bad, or the wrong one. Wouldn't be the first time thats happened.<p>since it only is happening occasionally, It's likely either blow by only when cold, or at high RPM, but it still could be the PCV screwy. The compression can test fine, but if the rings are starting to fail they will show signs first at high RPM and when cold.
Z||000006||overlord||08-24-99||07:26 PM||||thanks guys...this problem was bugging the heck out o me..<p>since the swap ..ive only put on a few hundred miles...i notice now that the oil is coming up less and less now...<p>u guys rock...as does these little beasts we call fieros.
